messages.reasoning_content:
ImageMagick is available. I'll create a hero image 285px tall (say 800x285) for the measurement harness in /tmp.

Note: In the deployed page, hero.png is 404, so the real live hero image is broken and renders as a small broken-image box (Chromium renders alt text / broken icon). The user asserts the hero image height on the rendered page is 285px. So the hero image in production exists (their real production has hero.png). Our local server lacks it. I'll use a 285px tall hero in my local measurement to reproduce the stated condition.

Measurement harness plan:
- /tmp/measure/index.html — copy of repo index.html
- /tmp/measure/assets/app.css — the fixed app.css (matching /assets/app.css path)
- /tmp/measure/hero.png — 800x285 image

Then run chromium headless with a JS snippet that reports `document.querySelector('.hero-img').getBoundingClientRect().height` and `.quote-panel` height, and write results to DOM, dump-dom.

Approach: use `--headless --dump-dom --virtual-time-budget=2000` on a page that runs script on load and puts measurements into document.title or a div. dump-dom runs the page (JS executes) after virtual time budget. Let's test.

Actually a cleaner way: use chromium's `--headless --remote-debugging-port` and CDP via node. Simpler is dump-dom. Let me write:

```html
<script>
window.onload = () => {
  const hero = document.querySelector('.hero-img').getBoundingClientRect();
  const quote = document.querySelector('.quote-panel').getBoundingClientRect();
  const out = document.getElementById('out');
  out.textContent = `HERO ${hero.height} QUOTE ${quote.height}`;
};
</script>
<div id="out">pending</div>
```

With `--dump-dom`, the DOM is serialized after the page loads (with virtual time budget allowing JS to run). Let's test it. Also need `--no-sandbox` (running as user, not root, in a container?). Use `--headless=new --disable-gpu --no-sandbox`.

Let's set up.

